 "note": {
  "status": "draft",
  "text": "The Middle Ages (about 500–1500) began after the fall of the Western Roman Empire in 476.\nThe early Middle Ages are sometimes called the Dark Ages: trade, towns and learning declined; the Church and its monasteries kept learning alive. Charlemagne was crowned emperor in 800.\nFeudalism: the king gave land (fiefs) to lords; lords gave land to knights, who fought for them; serfs (peasants) worked the land of the manor and could not leave it.\nOther events: the Crusades (1096–1291), wars to capture Jerusalem; the Black Death (1347–1351) killed about a third of Europe's people.\nThe Renaissance (“rebirth”, 14th–16th centuries) began in Italy (Florence): new interest in the learning of Greece and Rome, art and science. Leonardo da Vinci and Michelangelo were great artists. Gutenberg's printing press (about 1450) spread books. It led to the voyages of discovery.",
  "checks": []
 },
 "worked_example": {
  "status": "draft",
  "vars": {
   "r": "a serf"
  },
  "problem": "Describe the life of {r} in the feudal system.",
  "steps": [
   "A serf worked the lord's land on the manor and gave part of the harvest to the lord.",
   "In return he got protection and a small plot, but he could not leave the manor."
  ],
  "answer": "He worked the lord's land for protection and could not leave the manor.",
  "checks": []
 },
